+- **When not to use:** High-fidelity offline rendering requiring accurate Mie scattering or polarized light simulation.
 
 ## Background
-The coefficients for classical phase functions were derived for efficient real-time rendering of atmospheric scattering. The specific algebraic forms for Isotropic, Rayleigh, Hazzy, and Murky functions are presented in [S1, unofficial]. The Hazzy and Murky functions are less standard than Isotropic and Rayleigh, likely serving as empirical or semi-analytical approximations for specific atmospheric conditions (haze and murky water/air) in the context of the source paper. The Rayleigh phase function itself is derived from dipole radiation theory [Parent].
+The coefficients for classical phase functions are derived from analytical solutions to the radiative transfer equation for specific scattering models. The Isotropic, Rayleigh, Hazzy, and Murky phase functions are approximations used in atmospheric scattering to model different types of particulate matter. The specific coefficients provided in [S1, unofficial] appear to be from a practical implementation for real-time rendering, possibly based on earlier work in thermal radiation or atmospheric physics. The source [S1, unofficial] cites J.L. [Last Name Missing], Thermal Radiation Heat Transfer, 3rd ed. Hemisphere Publishing, 1992, as a reference for the underlying physics, but the specific coefficients for Hazzy and Murky seem to be tailored for computer graphics applications.
 
